  "source_texts": [
    "The Faith of the Centurion\n1 When __PERSON_F__ had finished saying all this to the people who were listening, he entered Capernaum. 2 There a centurion’s servant, whom his master valued highly, was sick and about to die. 3 The centurion heard of __PERSON_F__ and sent some elders of the Jews to him, asking him to come and heal his servant. 4 When they came to __PERSON_F__, they pleaded earnestly with him, “This man deserves to have you do this, 5 because he loves our nation and has built our synagogue.” 6 So __PERSON_F__ went with them.\n\nHe was not far from the house when the centurion sent friends to say to him: “__MASTER_A__, don’t trouble yourself, for I do not deserve to have you come under my roof. 7 That is why I did not even consider myself worthy to come to you. But say the word, and my servant will be healed. 8 For I myself am a man under authority, with soldiers under me. I tell this one, ‘Go,’ and he goes; and that one, ‘Come,’ and he comes. I say to my servant, ‘Do this,’ and he does it.”\n\n9 When __PERSON_F__ heard this, he was amazed at him, and turning to the crowd following him, he said, “I tell you, I have not found such great faith even in __PEOPLE_A__.” 10 Then the men who had been sent returned to the house and found the servant well.\n\n__PERSON_F__ Raises a Widow’s Son\n11 Soon afterward, __PERSON_F__ went to a town called Nain, and his disciples and a large crowd went along with him. 12 As he approached the town gate, a dead person was being carried out—the only son of his mother, and she was a widow. And a large crowd from the town was with her. 13 When the __MASTER_A__ saw her, his heart went out to her and he said, “Don’t cry.”\n\n14 Then he went up and touched the bier they were carrying him on, and the bearers stood still. He said, “Young man, I say to you, get up!” 15 The dead man sat up and began to talk, and __PERSON_F__ gave him back to his mother.\n\n16 They were all filled with awe and praised __MOST_HIGH_A__. “A great prophet has appeared among us,” they said. “__MOST_HIGH_A__ has come to help his people.” 17 This news about __PERSON_F__ spread throughout Judea and the surrounding country.\n\n__PERSON_F__ and __PERSON_C__ the Baptist\n18 __PERSON_C__ disciples told him about all these things. Calling two of them, 19 he sent them to the __MASTER_A__ to ask, “Are you the one who is to come, or should we expect someone else?”\n\n20 When the men came to __PERSON_F__, they said, “__PERSON_C__ the Baptist sent us to you to ask, ‘Are you the one who is to come, or should we expect someone else?’”\n\n21 At that very time __PERSON_F__ cured many who had diseases, sicknesses and evil spirits, and gave sight to many who were blind. 22 So he replied to the messengers, “Go back and report to __PERSON_C__ what you have seen and heard: The blind receive sight, the lame walk, those who have leprosy[a] are cleansed, the deaf hear, the dead are raised, and the good news is proclaimed to the poor. 23 Blessed is anyone who does not stumble on account of me.”\n\n24 After __PERSON_C__ messengers left, __PERSON_F__ began to speak to the crowd about __PERSON_C__: “What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed swayed by the wind? 25 If not, what did you go out to see? A man dressed in fine clothes? No, those who wear expensive clothes and indulge in luxury are in palaces. 26 But what did you go out to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet. 27 This is the one about whom it is written:\n\n“‘I will send my messenger ahead of you,\nwho will prepare your way before you.’[b]\n\n28 I tell you, among those born of women there is no one greater than __PERSON_C__; yet the one who is least in the kingdom of __MOST_HIGH_A__ is greater than he.”\n\n29 (All the people, even the tax collectors, when they heard __PERSON_F__ words, acknowledged that __MOST_HIGH_A__ way was right, because they had been baptized by __PERSON_C__. 30 But the Pharisees and the experts in the law rejected __MOST_HIGH_A__ purpose for themselves, because they had not been baptized by __PERSON_C__.)\n\n31 __PERSON_F__ went on to say, “To what, then, can I compare the people of this generation? What are they like? 32 They are like children sitting in the marketplace and calling out to each other:\n\n“‘We played the pipe for you,\nand you did not dance;\nwe sang a dirge,\nand you did not cry.’\n\n33 For __PERSON_C__ the Baptist came neither eating bread nor drinking wine, and you say, ‘He has a demon.’ 34 The Son of Man came eating and drinking, and you say, ‘Here is a glutton and a drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners.’ 35 But wisdom is proved right by all her children.”\n\n__PERSON_F__ Anointed by a Sinful Woman\n36 When one of the Pharisees invited __PERSON_F__ to have dinner with him, he went to the Pharisee’s house and reclined at the table. 37 A woman in that town who lived a sinful life learned that __PERSON_F__ was eating at the Pharisee’s house, so she came there with an alabaster jar of perfume. 38 As she stood behind him at his feet weeping, she began to wet his feet with her tears. Then she wiped them with her hair, kissed them and poured perfume on them.\n\n39 When the Pharisee who had invited him saw this, he said to himself, “If this man were a prophet, he would know who is touching him and what kind of woman she is—that she is a sinner.”\n\n40 __PERSON_F__ answered him, “Simon, I have something to tell you.”\n\n“Tell me, teacher,” he said.\n\n41 “Two people owed money to a certain moneylender. One owed him five hundred denarii,[c] and the other fifty. 42 Neither of them had the money to pay him back, so he forgave the debts of both. Now which of them will love him more?”\n\n43 Simon replied, “I suppose the one who had the bigger debt forgiven.”\n\n“You have judged correctly,” __PERSON_F__ said.\n\n44 Then he turned toward the woman and said to Simon, “Do you see this woman? I came into your house. You did not give me any water for my feet, but she wet my feet with her tears and wiped them with her hair. 45 You did not give me a kiss, but this woman, from the time I entered, has not stopped kissing my feet. 46 You did not put oil on my head, but she has poured perfume on my feet. 47 Therefore, I tell you, her many sins have been forgiven—as her great love has shown. But whoever has been forgiven little loves little.”\n\n48 Then __PERSON_F__ said to her, “Your sins are forgiven.”\n\n49 The other guests began to say among themselves, “Who is this who even forgives sins?”\n\n50 __PERSON_F__ said to the woman, “Your faith has saved you; go in peace.”\n"
  ],
  "translations": [
    "百夫长的信心  \n1 当 __PERSON_F__ 对听众说完这一切话后，就进入了迦百农。2 那里有一个百夫长的仆人，他深受主人器重，病得快要死了。3 百夫长听说了 __PERSON_F__，便派犹太人的几位长老去见他，请他来治愈他的仆人。4 当他们到 __PERSON_F__ 面前时，极力恳求他说：“这个人配得你帮他，5 因为他爱我们的民族，又为我们建造了会堂。”6 于是 __PERSON_F__ 就同他们去了。\n\n他还没到那人家的时候，百夫长派朋友对他说：“__MASTER_A__，请不要麻烦您，我不配让您进我的屋子。7 所以我连亲自来见您都不敢自称配得。但只要您一句话，我的仆人就会痊愈。8 因为我自己也是属上级权柄的人，手下有士兵。我对这一个说‘去’，他就去；对那一个说‘来’，他就来。我对我的仆人说‘做’，他就做。”  \n\n9 __PERSON_F__ 听了这话，就很惊讶他，转向跟随他的人群说：“我告诉你们，连在 __PEOPLE_A__ 中我也未曾遇见如此大的信心。”10 派去的人回到那家，见仆人已经好了。\n\n__PERSON_F__ 使寡妇的儿子复活  \n11 不久，__PERSON_F__ 到了一个名叫拿因的城，他的门徒和许多人跟随他去。12 将近城门的时候，有人抬着一具死人，是一个寡妇的独生子，镇上很大的人群与她同行。13 __MASTER_A__ 看见她，就怜悯她，说：“不要哭。”  \n\n14 于是走上前去，摸抬死人用的床，抬他的几个人就停住了。他说：“年轻人，我吩咐你，起来！”15 那死人就坐起来，开始说话，__PERSON_F__ 把他交还给母亲。  \n\n16 众人都惊奇赞美 __MOST_HIGH_A__ ，说：“我们中间兴起了一位大先知，__MOST_HIGH_A__ 来帮助他的百姓了。”17 关于 __PERSON_F__ 的消息在犹太和周围各地都传开了。\n\n__PERSON_F__ 与施洗约翰 __PERSON_C__  \n18 __PERSON_C__ 的门徒将这些事告诉他。19 他叫来了两个人，派他们去见 __MASTER_A__ ，问他说：“你就是那要来的，还是我们还要等候别人？”  \n\n20 那两人来到 __PERSON_F__ 面前，说：“施洗约翰 __PERSON_C__ 打发我们来问你：‘你是那要来的，还是我们还要等候别人？’”  \n\n21 当时，__PERSON_F__ 治好了许多有疾病、残疾和恶灵的人，也使许多盲人能看见。22 他回答使者说：“你们回去，把所看见所听见的都告诉 __PERSON_C__ ：盲人看见了，瘸子行走了，麻风病得洁净，聋子听见了，死人复活了，穷人有福音传给他们。23 凡不因我跌倒的有福了。”  \n\n24 __PERSON_C__ 的使者走后，__PERSON_F__ 就开始向众人谈论 __PERSON_C__ ：“你们去旷野是要看什么？是被风吹动的芦苇吗？25 不是的话，你们去看什么？穿华丽衣服的人吗？不，那些穿华丽衣服、过奢侈生活的人都在王宫里。26 那你们去看什么？先知吗？是的，我告诉你们，他不只是个先知。27 这正是经上所记：  \n\n‘我要差我的使者在你前面，  \n为你预备道路。’[b]  \n\n28 我告诉你们，在女人所生的，没有比 __PERSON_C__ 更大的；然而在 __MOST_HIGH_A__ 的国里最小的比他还大。”  \n\n29 (众人甚至税吏听 __PERSON_F__ 说的话，都承认 __MOST_HIGH_A__ 的道路是正的，因为他们已经接受了 __PERSON_C__ 的洗礼。30 法利赛人和律法师却自己拒绝 __MOST_HIGH_A__ 的旨意，因为他们没有接受 __PERSON_C__ 的洗礼。)  \n\n31 __PERSON_F__ 又说：“那么，这世代的人该比作什么呢？他们像什么呢？32 他们像坐在市场上的孩子，互相呼叫说：  \n\n‘我们为你们吹笛，  \n你们却不跳舞；  \n我们唱哀歌，  \n你们却不哭泣。’  \n\n33 因为施洗约翰 __PERSON_C__ 不吃饭也不喝酒，你们就说‘他是被鬼附着的’，34 人子来了，吃喝，你们又说‘看哪，他是贪吃好酒的人，是税吏和罪人的朋友。’35 但智慧人用她的行为证明自己是对的。”  \n\n罪恶的女人膏抹 __PERSON_F__  \n36 有一位法利赛人请 __PERSON_F__ 吃饭，他进了法利赛人的家，在席上坐下。37 那城有一个行淫的女人，知道 __PERSON_F__ 在法利赛人家里吃饭，拿着一瓶膏药来到那里。38 她站在他背后，靠近脚哭泣，用眼泪润湿他的脚，又用头发擦干，亲他的脚，倾倒香膏在脚上。  \n\n39 那请他吃饭的法利赛人看见，就心里说：“若这人是先知，必知道摸他的是怎样的女人，是个罪人。”  \n\n40 __PERSON_F__ 对他说：“西门，我有话要告诉你。”  \n\n“请说，老师，”他说。  \n\n41 “有两个人欠一个债主债，一个欠五百银币[c]，另一个欠五十。42 他们都没有钱还债，债主就免了他们的债。现在你说，哪个人会更爱他？”  \n\n43 西门回答说：“我想是债务较多的那一个。”  \n\n“你断得不错，” __PERSON_F__ 说。  \n\n44 他转向那女人，对西门说：“你看见这女人了吗？我进你家，你没有给我水洗脚，她却用眼泪湿了我的脚，又用头发擦干。45 你没有给我亲嘴，她从我进来起就不断亲我的脚。46 你没有膏我的头，他却用香膏膏我的脚。47 所以我告诉你，她许多的罪都赦免了，因为她爱得多；赦免得少的，爱也少。”  \n\n48 __PERSON_F__ 对她说：“你的罪赦免了。”  \n\n49 其他人就彼此说：“这是谁，连罪也赦免呢？”  \n\n50 __PERSON_F__ 对女人说：“你的信救了你，平平安安地回去吧。”"
  ],
